  "text": "All files verified. Everything is consistent: HTML IDs match the `els` lookups, every CSS class used in markup/JS exists in the stylesheet, and both the JS and CSS are brace-balanced.\n\n**Note on validation:** this environment only exposes file read/search/edit tools — there is no shell/execution tool available, so I could not run the app or a linter. I validated statically instead, tracing each interaction end-to-end (add → tick → toggle past day → streak transitions → reload → delete) and checking that every `getElementById`/`querySelector` target exists in `index.html` and every class used in JS/HTML has a rule in `styles.css`.\n\n---\n\n## What I built\n\nFour files in `/private/tmp/.../work/apps`:\n\n| File | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `index.html` | Markup + a `<template>` for one habit row |\n| `styles.css` | Styling; light/dark via `prefers-color-scheme` |\n| `app.js` | Date helpers, persistence, streaks, rendering, events |\n| `README.md` | How to run, data format, streak rules |\n\nVanilla HTML/CSS/JS with **zero dependencies and no build step** — the workspace was completely empty (no `package.json`, no framework, no tooling), so a static app was the only choice that works without installing anything.\n\n### Your requirements, and how each is met\n\n- **Add habits** — text input + button, Enter also submits. Trimmed, whitespace-collapsed, capped at 60 chars, case-insensitive duplicate rejected with an inline message.\n- **Tick off for today** — a round **Mark done / Done today** pill per habit (green with a check when done).\n- **Last 7 days** — a 7-cell strip per habit, oldest → today, showing weekday initial + day number. Today has a ring. Cells are clickable so you can correct a missed day (a superset of what you asked for).\n- **Streak count** — shown under each habit name, e.g. `3 days in a row`.\n- **Remembers everything** — `localStorage` under `habit-tracker.v1`. Reload, close the tab, come back tomorrow: all there.\n- **Delete a habit** — a Delete button with a confirm dialog.\n\n### Streak rule I chose (worth knowing)\n\nA streak is the run of consecutive completed days **ending today**. If today isn't ticked yet, the run is measured from *yesterday*, so an unfinished today doesn't wipe out your streak — instead the habit shows `3 days in a row · not done today` so it's clear the streak is at risk. Ticking today then makes it `4 days in a row`. This is the friendlier convention (Habitica/Streak style) rather than the harsher \"miss today and you're back to zero\". It's documented in the README.\n\n### Details worth flagging\n\n- **Local calendar days, not UTC** — dates are built with the local-time `Date` constructor and keyed as `YYYY-MM-DD`, so \"today\" always matches your clock rather than drifting by timezone offset.\n- **`log` is sparse** — only completed days are stored, so the payload stays tiny.\n- **Defensive loading** — stored data is re-validated on load: unknown fields dropped, malformed date keys ignored, unusable records skipped, and a corrupt JSON payload falls back to an empty list instead of breaking the page.\n- **XSS-safe** — habit names go through `textContent`, never `innerHTML`, so a name like `<img onerror=...>` renders as literal text.\n- **Graceful `localStorage` failure** — if storage is blocked (Safari on `file://` does this), the app still works but shows a banner explaining that nothing will be saved and suggesting `python3 -m http.server`. This is the one thing that could surprise you, which is why I detect it up front rather than silently losing data.\n- **Focus is preserved** across re-renders — the list re-renders wholesale after each change, so the control you used is refocused. Otherwise keyboard/screen-reader use would be jarring.\n- **Tab left open overnight** re-renders on `visibilitychange`, so it rolls over to the new day instead of showing yesterday's state.\n- **Cross-tab sync** via the `storage` event.\n- **Dark mode**, responsive layout (row reflows under 430px), and `prefers-reduced-motion` respected.\n\n### To run it\n\n```sh\ncd /private/tmp/.../work/apps\npython3 -m http.server 8000   # then open http://localhost:8000\n```\n\nOr just open `index.html` directly (with the caveat above about Safari + `file://`).\n\n### Assumptions I made\n\n- \"Today\" means your device's local date, not a server date.\n- Deleting a habit is permanent — the confirm dialog is the safety net. I deliberately did not add undo, since you didn't ask for it and it would have added complexity; say the word and I can add an undo toast instead.\n- Habit history is unbounded (no archiving/retention limit).\n- No editing of a habit's name after creation — delete and re-add covers it. Happy to add inline rename if you want it.",
